  "_style": "Write warm, natural standard Shona (chiShona, Zezuru-based standard orthography), never stiff or slangy. Always address the user in the singular “iwe” (u-/-ako: “Tumira tsamba kumudiwa wako”); address the couple in the plural “imi” (ma-/-enyu: “muti wenyu”, “makabatana”) and call them “imi vaviri” or “pamwe chete”; the app speaks of itself sparingly. The other person is always “mudiwa wako” (never mukomana/musikana, murume/mukadzi); Shona has no grammatical gender, so use class-1 pronouns (iye, a-, -ake) and avoid gendered nouns. A partner's name placeholder (%@) is never prefixed or inflected (no “kuna%@”, “na%@”, “wa%@”, “va%@”): rephrase so the name stands alone, e.g. “%@ akutumira tsamba” or “Mudiwa wako (%@)”; likewise never attach concords or prefixes to numbers or other placeholders. Use curly double quotes “ ” (inner ‘ ’), the single ellipsis character …, no space before ? ! : ;, and keep emoji, \\n and the “ · ” separator exactly. Loanwords are spelled the Shona way (akaundi, purofaira, korona) but brand and product names stay as is (Coupley, CoupleSync, Premium, AI); attach concords to them without a hyphen only when unavoidable (weAI), otherwise rephrase. Numbers use Western digits; dates follow the system format (e.g. 14 Mbudzi 2026) and never get hand-written prefixes. Buttons and actions are short imperatives (Chengeta, Tumira, Edza zvakare, Vhara nechisimbiso); titles, tabs and buttons use sentence case (capital on the first word only) except proper names. Latin script only. Plural categories are [\"one\", \"other\"]: put the number first and use the singular noun for “one” (“%lld zuva” / “%lld mazuva”, “%lld tsamba” / “%lld tsamba”). Tone: loving, light and kind, never cheesy or preachy.",
